Bug: a header held at an exit keeps the buffer's references after a restart

On -exit, forgetKnowledgeForHeldContent() (src/cxref.c) only zeroes the
knowledge time of held files. The references from the unsaved buffer are
still in the table and are written to the snapshot. For a CU that is enough,
since it is then out of date and its build removes its own references first.
A header's references are removed by removeReferencesOfChangedFiles()
(src/rebuild.c), which compares changeTime with contentTimeOf() and never
reads the knowledge time. While held, changeTime was taken from the buffer,
i.e. the preload's time, which is later than the disk's, and it is saved. After
the restart the disk is not newer, so the header is not seen to change, nothing
is built, and shared is found on line 2 where the buffer had it.

Likely fix: ADR-0036 (#112). A held file's seen token is dropped when the
snapshot is written, so it counts as changed after a restart, header or CU
alike, and forgetKnowledgeForHeldContent() goes. A quicker fix within today's
times would be to zero changeTime for held files at -exit as well.

The snapshot is also written every 100 CUs during a build (src/rebuild.c),
without forgetKnowledgeForHeldContent(), so a crash after such a save may leave
the same for a held CU. Not tested.

Found by reading the code, confirmed by this test and a trace of both sessions
(WSL, session d25255b8, 2026-11-08).
